{
  "id": "Compound::DB01274 - Disease::DOID:2841",
  "verbalization": "Arformoterol, an adrenergic beta2-agonist, is linked to Asthma through a shared biological pathway involving phenylethanolamines and beta-adrenergic agonists. The evidence suggests that Arformoterol, as part of the broader class of adrenergic beta2-agonists, is associated with Pirbuterol and Orciprenaline, both of which treat Asthma. This indicates a potential mechanism where these compounds exert their therapeutic effects through similar pathways, possibly by stimulating beta2-adrenergic receptors in bronchial smooth muscle, leading to bronchodilation and reduced airway inflammation. The presence of a common ancestor in the phenylethanolamine class further supports a shared biochemical mechanism, suggesting that Arformoterol may act through a similar mechanism as its relatives in the adrenergic beta2-agonist family. The evidence highlights a convergent pathway where Arformoterol, through its inclusion in the adrenergic beta2-agonist class, may contribute to the treatment of asthma via a mechanism involving beta-adrenergic receptor activation.",
